Moving to a json presents a few issues; but a much cleaner config.
Ideally there wouldn't be two files necessary for this plugin to be
useful. Defining custom functions is what makes this useful, so we
need to find a way to trigger commands for the concept of something
like 'branch create' that still allows for a UI like gum or fzf.

One option is to allow for including shell scripts that can then be
called by the plugin.

{
  "include": ["$HOME/.projectrc/", "./.project.sh"]
  "branch": {
    "route-key": "^g",
    "description": "Manage git branches",
    "binding": {
      "c": "project_branch_create",
      "s": "project_branch_switch",
      "r": "project_branch_rebase"
    }
  },
  "build": {
    "description": "Build the project",
    "binding": {
      "b": "npm run build"
    }
  },
  "start": {
    "description": "Start development server",
    "binding": {
      "s": "npm start"
    }
  }
}

{
  "include": ["$HOME/.projectrc/", "./.project.sh"]
  "prefix": "^p",
  "binding": {
    "a": "app",
    "b": "branch",
    "c": "config",
  },
  "branch": {
    "prefix": "^g",
    "description": "Manage git branches",
    "binding": {
      "c": "project_branch_create",
      "s": "project_branch_switch",
      "r": "project_branch_rebase"
    }
  },
  "app": {
    "build": {
      "description": "Build the project",
      "binding": {
        "b": "npm run build"
      }
    },
    "start": {
      "description": "Start development server",
      "binding": {
        "s": "npm start"
      }
    }
  },
  "config": {
    "binding: {
      "e": "EDITOR=nvim ./project.json",
    },
  }
}



To move those specific commits while keeping their order and messages intact, the git cherry-pick range command is exactly what you need.

Since your oldest commit is a5ffbed7 and your newest is a2054a73, follow these steps from your target branch:

The Range Cherry-Pick
Run this command to pull the entire block of work over:

Bash
git cherry-pick a5ffbed7^..a2054a73
Why the ^ is important
In Git range syntax (A..B), the first commit is usually excluded. Adding the caret ^ tells Git to include the starting commit itself. Without it, you would miss the "Create a component..." commit.

Step-by-Step Workflow
Ensure your target branch is clean: Before starting, make sure you don't have uncommitted changes that might conflict.

Execute the pick: Run the command above. Git will replay each commit one by one in chronological order.

Handle Conflicts (If any): If a file you modified in these commits has changed differently on the target branch, Git will pause.

Fix the file.

git add <file>

git cherry-pick --continue

Verify: Run git log --oneline -n 3 to see your beautiful, preserved commit history on the new branch.

A "Project Port" Tool for your CLI
Since you already have a project undo interface (seen in your screenshots), you can build a project port function that uses that same gum style to pick a range and automate this.

Would you like me to write a function that lets you select a "Start" and "End" commit from a list and runs this cherry-pick for you?


The target branch origin and my my local copy have diverged, which makes sense because the remote branch has been forced pushed to. Do I just run 'git pull' or is there another step to take before cherry picking?

Since the remote branch has been force-pushed, a standard git pull will likely result in a messy merge commit or a "refusing to merge unrelated histories" error. To get your local copy in sync with the new reality of the remote branch before you start cherry-picking, you should use git reset.

The Pre-Cherry-Pick Workflow
You need to align your local branch pointer exactly with the remote's new position. Run these commands while on your target branch:

Fetch the latest state: git fetch origin This updates your local knowledge of what origin/branch-name looks like without touching your working files.

Force-align your local branch: git reset --hard origin/<target-branch-name> This discards your local diverged history and makes your local branch an identical twin of the remote. Warning: Ensure you don't have local work on this branch you want to keep, as --hard will wipe uncommitted changes.

Why not git pull?
When a remote is force-pushed, the commit history has been rewritten. If you pull, Git tries to "unify" your old local history with the new remote history. Since they share a common ancestor but have different paths, you'll end up with a "Merge branch..." commit that includes all the old versions of the commits you're trying to get rid of.

The Full Command Sequence
Once your target branch is reset and clean, you can proceed with the cherry-pick:

Bash
# 1. Sync the target branch
git fetch origin
git checkout <target-branch>
git reset --hard origin/<target-branch>

# 2. Apply your range of commits
git cherry-pick a5ffbed7^..a2054a73
Handling the "Moved" Commits
Since you are moving these commits to this branch, they will now appear as brand new commits on top of the reset history.
